Use an in-order traversal with pruning: if node.v < L, skip the entire left subtree; if node.v > R, skip the entire right subtree. Otherwise include node.v and recurse both sides. Pseudocode:

```python
def range_sum(t, L, R):
    if not t: return 0
    if t.v < L:
        return range_sum(t.r, L, R)  # all left < L
    if t.v > R:
        return range_sum(t.l, L, R)  # all right > R
    return t.v + range_sum(t.l, L, R) + range_sum(t.r, L, R)
```
Complexity: Best case (tight pruning, balanced-ish or keys outside range) is O(h + k) where h is height and k is number of nodes in [L,R]; with a balanced tree h=O(log n). Worst case (all nodes in range or a skewed tree) degrades to O(n). Space is O(h) recursion depth (or O(h) if implemented iteratively with a stack).
